\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Matrix units give the even-dimensional structure theorem}

\p{Let #{K} be a field of characteristic different from #{2}, and let #{V}
be a finite-dimensional split quadratic space with a chosen polarization
#{V=W\oplus W'} into complementary isotropic subspaces. Put
#{\dim_KW=\ell}, choose polar-dual bases #{e_i\in W} and #{e_i'\in W'} with
#{B_Q(e_i,e_j')=\delta_{ij}}, and set #{S=\bigwedge W}. In the notation of
\ref{fcap-0027}, write

##{c_i=\varepsilon_{e_i},\qquad
 a_i=a_{e_i'},\qquad N_i=c_i a_i,\qquad
 P_0=\prod_i(1-N_i)=\prod_i a_i c_i.}

The commuting idempotents #{N_i} record occupation, so #{P_0} is the
projection onto the vacuum line #{K1}. For increasing subsets
#{I=\{i_1<\cdots<i_p\}} and #{J=\{j_1<\cdots<j_q\}}, put

##{c_I=c_{i_1}\cdots c_{i_p},\qquad
 a_J=a_{j_q}\cdots a_{j_1},\qquad
 F_{I,J}=c_IP_0a_J.}

The reversed contraction order is essential. If #{e_K} denotes the
increasing exterior basis vector, then

##{F_{I,J}(e_K)=\delta_{J,K}e_I,\qquad
 F_{I,J}F_{K,L}=\delta_{J,K}F_{I,L}.}

Indeed, #{a_J(e_J)=1}. A missing index makes the contraction zero, while a
properly larger #{K} leaves positive exterior degree and is killed by
#{P_0}. Finally #{c_I(1)=e_I}. Thus the #{F_{I,J}} are all matrix units in
the image of the Clifford action. This is the multimode extension of the
rank-one calculation in \ref{fcap-001P}.}

\p{The matrix units prove surjectivity, and the dimension equality

##{\dim_K\Cl(Q)=2^{2\ell}=(2^\ell)^2
 =\dim_K\operatorname{End}_K(S)}

then proves injectivity. Hence

##{\Cl(Q)\simeq\operatorname{End}_K(S).}

This full equivalence is formalized by
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.spinAction_bijective} and
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.cliffordEquivEnd}. The
displayed multi-index matrix units are the basis calculation supplied here,
not a separately named declaration.}

\p{Every factor in #{P_0} is even, so the Clifford parity of #{F_{I,J}} is
#{|I|+|J|} modulo #{2}. The even matrix units are therefore exactly those
with #{|I|\equiv|J|\pmod{2}}; they span the two diagonal blocks and give

##{\Cl(Q)^0\simeq
 \operatorname{End}_K(S^+)\oplus\operatorname{End}_K(S^-),\qquad
 S^+=\bigwedge^{\mathrm{even}}W,\quad
 S^-=\bigwedge^{\mathrm{odd}}W.}

The exact formal block equivalence is
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.evenCliffordEquivProdEnd}.
If #{\ell\geq1}, each block has size #{2^{\ell-1}} and each parity summand
is simple for its full endomorphism block
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.evenCliffordEquivProdMatrix}.
For #{\ell=0}, the full action still identifies #{K} with
#{\operatorname{End}_K(K)}, but #{S^-=0} and is not called a simple
summand.}

\p{At the group level, the block calculation alone does not prove
irreducibility after restriction to #{\Spin(Q)}. When #{K} is additionally
separably closed and #{\ell\geq1}, the formal Spin-spanning argument gives
the two irreducible half-spin representations
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.isIrreducible_spinPlusSubrep}
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.isIrreducible_spinMinusSubrep}.
This is stronger input than the occupation weights of \ref{fcap-0025}.}

\p{\citet{Lemma 20.9 and (20.13), pp. 304--305}{fulton2004representation}
derive the full complex action and its two exterior-parity blocks; their
bilinear form is the half-polar form #{b_Q}, so their contraction coefficient
#{2Q_{\mathrm{FH}}} is our #{B_Q}.}

\p{\citet{Section 3.2.4, Theorem 3.3 and (3.5)--(3.6), pp. 59--60}{meinrenken2013clifford}
give the split action as a superalgebra isomorphism and prove it by the
rank-one tensor calculation; their creation space #{F^*} is our #{W} and
their bilinear form is #{b_Q}.}

\p{\citet{Theorem 6.1.3, proof, pp. 304--305}{goodman2009symmetry}
independently obtain the complex creation--contraction carrier; their
creation space #{W_{\mathrm{GW}}^*} is our #{W}, and their form #{\beta} is
our unhalved #{B_Q}. The formula #{F_{I,J}=c_IP_0a_J} synthesizes these
arguments in the fixed FCAP order rather than quoting one source verbatim.}
